  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Diplocarpa irregularis is a rare flowering plant belonging to the Cordieritidaceae family, endemic to the New England region of North America. This small herbaceous plant is notable for its distinctive paired fruit structures and delicate floral characteristics.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Paired Fruit Structure",
      "description": "The most distinctive feature is the paired or doubled carpels that give the genus its name (Diplo- meaning two), creating an irregular fruiting pattern unique among regional flora.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaf Arrangement",
      "description": "Look for alternately arranged, narrow leaves with a slightly waxy texture and prominent venation that helps distinguish it from similar species.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flower Characteristics",
      "description": "Small, delicate flowers with four to five petals that appear in late spring, typically ranging from pale pink to white in coloration.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Diplocarpa irregularis thrives in well-drained, sandy woodland soils and rocky slopes of New England, often found in association with oak and pine forests at moderate elevations.",
